    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d730-a1">

                <pb facs="0956" n="860" sameAs="frus:frus1898/pg860"/>

                <p rendition="#flush">1898.</p>
                <p rendition="#right">No. 9.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">publication sheet.</hi>
                </p>
                <p rendition="#flush"><hi rendition="#italic">Ordinance supplementing article 20 of
                        the ordinance of July 28–December 20, 1881 “containing an amendment of the
                        regulations in force concerning the importation, transit, and exportation of
                        merchandise in the island of Curaçao, and the proof of the collection of the
                        fire and light-house dues there.”</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Publication
                        Sheet No. 22.</hi>)</p>
                <p>In the Queen’s name: The governor of Curaçao, considering that it is necessary to
                    supplement article 20 of the ordinance of July 28–December 20, 1881, “containing
                    an amendment of the regulations in force concerning the importation, transit,
                    and exportation of merchandise in the island of Curaçao, and the proof of the
                    collection of the lire and light-house dues there” (Publication Sheet No. 22),
                    has, with the approbation of the colonial council, and by virtue of the second
                    section of article 50 of the governmental regulations of this colony, decreed
                    the following ordinance:</p>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Article</hi> 1. The following is added to article 20
                    of the ordinance of July 28–December 20, 1881 (Publication Sheet No. 22), as a
                    sixth paragraph:</p>
                <p>“The transshipment of gunpowder, ammunition, firearms, side arms, or other war
                    materials may be prohibited from time to time by decree of the governor.”</p>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Art</hi>. 2. This ordinance goes into effect on the
                    day of its promulgation.</p>
                <closer>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-left">Given at <placeName>Willemstad</placeName>,
                            <date calendar="gregorian" when="1898-06-04">June 4,
                        1898</date>.</dateline>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Barge.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>Promulgated June 4, 1898.</p>
                    <closer>
                        <signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hellmund</hi>, <hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Secretary.</hi></signed>
                    </closer>
                </postscript>
            </div>
